WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.434
A secret has a lifecycle: creation, distribution, activation, use,
expiration, rotation, revocation, retirement, and emergency replacement.

2
00:00:08.434 --> 00:00:14.692
Managing that lifecycle is what separates mature secrets management from
merely storing a value somewhere.

3
00:00:14.692 --> 00:00:23.874
Every important secret should have an owner, a purpose, an environment, a
storage location, consumers, rotation expectations, and an incident
response path.

4
00:00:23.874 --> 00:00:29.451
Rotation changes or replaces a secret according to time, risk, exposure,
or operational policy.

5
00:00:29.451 --> 00:00:33.396
Rotation must be planned because secrets connect systems together.

6
00:00:33.396 --> 00:00:42.646
A database password, signing key, webhook secret, or API credential may be
used by multiple services, scheduled jobs, deployment tools, and support
processes.

7
00:00:42.646 --> 00:00:46.319
Changing it without coordination can cause avoidable outages.

8
00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:49.448
Not changing it after exposure can extend compromise.

9
00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:53.053
Dual-secret or staged rotation concepts can reduce downtime.

10
00:00:53.053 --> 00:01:02.031
For example, a service may temporarily accept both old and new credentials
while clients update, or a provider may allow two active keys during a
transition.

11
00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:08.357
The exact mechanism depends on the system, but the stable principle is to
design rotation before an emergency.

12
00:01:08.357 --> 00:01:14.002
Service restart and reload behavior should be understood so new secrets
take effect predictably.

13
00:01:14.002 --> 00:01:17.267
Revocation and retirement are as important as creation.

14
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:27.469
When a secret is exposed, retired, no longer needed, or associated with a
departed vendor, employee, service, or environment, it should be disabled
or removed according to risk.

15
00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:33.047
Documentation and inventory help teams know what to rotate, who depends on
it, and how to recover.

16
00:01:33.047 --> 00:01:38.760
Rotation should improve security without surprising the people who keep
the application running.
